.TITLE "ls"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
ls is a tool used to **see the contents** of a folder.

You can think of it like turning on the lights in a room to see what is on the shelves. 
It helps you **know where you are** and what files you can work with.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
ls

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**ls** lists the names of files and folders in your current location.

Here, __ls__ is the tool. 
It gives you a quick overview of what is in the folder so you can **decide what to do next**.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
ls -l

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**ls -l** displays your files in a **detailed list**.

Here, __-l__ is a flag for __long format__. 
It shows you more than just names, like __how big a file is__ or __who owns it__.

This is useful when you need to **inspect file details** or check permissions.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
ls -a

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**ls -a** shows **every file** in the folder, even hidden ones.

Here, __-a__ stands for __all__. 
In Linux, some files start with a __.__ and are usually kept out of sight.

This is useful when you need to **find settings** or configuration files that are normally hidden.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
ls is your primary way to **look around** your system.
Always **use ls** when you enter a new folder to **orient yourself** and see what is available.
